  "hypotheses": [
    "A fixed portable provider-output envelope can retain an exact conventional 256-byte PCI configuration capture or a closed failure, while a separate semantic envelope independently retains normalized provider, PCI structure, and FLR facts.",
    "Provider identities and coveredDeviceUids can be reconstructed from the complete candidate DeviceRecord provenance set, and the union of PCI-specific bindings can be required to form an exact UID/BDF bijection with that record set.",
    "Raw provider bytes, semantic bytes, DeviceRecord V3 facts, topology/provider projections, and the no-change projection can all be independently replayed after every outer hash is recomputed, so digest-only labels and caller-selected projections fail closed.",
    "The current capability-policy hash and freshness-policy hash can be bound independently into the candidate no-change projection, while mandatory provider-fact presence and integer-microsecond evidence age are checked against the capture interval.",
    "A component algorithm identity must bind the exact ordered transitive schema set and validation stages; a root publication-schema digest alone cannot prevent schema-set or independently verified policy substitution.",
    "Shape/count and encoded/decoded byte, node, depth, provider-fact, and dependency-edge limits can run before expensive schema validation, while indexed uniqueness, freshness, and dependency reciprocity avoid adversarial quadratic or cubic scans.",
    "Candidate DeviceRecord rows can be made internally composable by requiring deviceGeneration to equal one shared candidateSnapshotEpoch and one shared candidateResourceVersion, while explicitly retaining that both coordinates are caller-stamped and still require repository CAS.",
    "A schema-valid factory receipt can prove component relationships without becoming trusted publication-time, target decoder, collector, repository, rollback-authority, visibility, or product authorization evidence."
  ],
